<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James IV of Scotland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1473">1473</a>–<a href="/years/1513">1513</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-stuart">House of Stuart</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James IV was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> from 1488 to 1513, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> ruler who strengthened royal authority, built a navy and patronised learning and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>. He forfeited the Lordship of the Isles in 1493 and married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-tudor">Margaret Tudor</a> in 1503. Honouring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auld-alliance">Auld Alliance</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and was killed with much of his nobility at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-flodden">Battle of Flodden</a> in 1513.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-iv-of-scotland.jpg" alt="James IV of Scotland" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_IV_of_Scotland_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>James was born on 17 March 1473 at Stirling Castle, the eldest son of King James III and Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, and was Duke of Rothesay from birth. He was educated by Archibald Whitelaw and the theologian John Ireland. Besides Scots he became fluent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Spanish, learned French, German, Flemish and Italian, and was the last Scottish monarch known to have spoken Gaelic. He became king at fifteen on 11 June 1488, when his father died at the Battle of Sauchieburn, after a rebellion in which he had been the figurehead of the rebels, and he was crowned at Scone Abbey on 24 June.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1491 Alexander MacDonald of Lochalsh raided Ross-shire in an attempt to recover the earldom of Ross. In consequence, a parliament in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> in May 1493 declared the title and possessions of John MacDonald, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-of-the-isles">Lord of the Isles</a>, forfeit to the Crown. James then made an expedition to the western Highlands, where local chiefs submitted, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-islay-earl-of-ross">John of Islay</a> was brought to court with an annual pension. Parliaments met ten times from 1488 to 1496 but only three times in the remaining seventeen years, and royal income doubled over the reign. The Treaty of Ayton of 30 September 1497 agreed a seven-year truce with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and the Treaty of Perpetual Peace followed in 1502, with James's marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-tudor">Margaret Tudor</a> in 1503.</p><p>He took a close interest in the navy, commissioning or acquiring at least thirty-eight ships, and naval spending became the largest single item of royal expenditure. He was a patron of the Scots poets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-dunbar">William Dunbar</a>, Walter Kennedy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gavin-douglas">Gavin Douglas</a>, backed the foundation of King's College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a>, and passed the education act of 1496, which required the eldest sons of barons and substantial freeholders to attend grammar school. His court was cosmopolitan and included Africans, among them two women mentioned in the treasurer's accounts in 1504. In 1507 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> granted him the title Protector and Defender of the Christian Faith.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1513 as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1511">Holy League</a>, James chose the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auld-alliance">Auld Alliance</a> with France over the Perpetual Peace and led an army of some 42,000 across the Tweed around 22 August. He took Norham, Etal and Ford castles, and on 8 September faced the English under Thomas Howard, Earl of Surrey, on Branxton Hill. James and many of his nobles were killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-flodden">Battle of Flodden</a> on 9 September 1513. His body was identified the next day, taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berwick-upon-tweed">Berwick-upon-Tweed</a> and embalmed. He was the last monarch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> to be killed in battle and was succeeded by his son James V.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James IV” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The alliance with France, the Auld Alliance first sealed in 1295, drew Scotland into repeated wars with England: <b>James IV</b> built a royal navy, including the great ship Michael, but was killed with much of his nobility at the Battle of Flodden in 1513, and another heavy defeat…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1493 <b>James IV</b> forfeited the lordship, and the title was later annexed to the heir of the Scottish crown in the Kingdom of Scotland.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-the-isles">Lordship of the Isles</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holyrood-abbey">Holyrood Abbey</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Branxton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.95,-3.17&amp;year=1499" aria-label="Holyrood Abbey on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of James IV of Scotland"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4848 5342h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4848 5342h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.95,-3.17&amp;year=1499">What was here in 1499? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
